# Epic workflow (Rolefit)

## Rule

Work in **epics**. When an epic is **complete**, do all of the following in the same turn:

1. Update `docs/EPICS.md` — mark the epic `done`, add ship notes (what / why / key paths).
2. Update `CHANGELOG.md` — short bullet(s) under today’s date for that epic only.
3. Keep code comments **useful and sparse** (module/purpose, non-obvious why) — no noisy line-by-line comments.
4. **Create one git commit** for that epic (do not wait for the user to ask). Push only if the user already asked to push or the session pattern has been push-on-continue.
5. Start or continue the next epic only after the commit succeeds.

## Commit message

Use a focused message that names the epic outcome, e.g.:

```
Complete epic: candidate directory search and pagination.

```

## While an epic is in progress

- Do **not** create drive-by commits for tiny intermediate edits.
- Keep `docs/EPICS.md` status as `in_progress` for the active epic.
- Prefer updating docs at epic end, not after every micro-change.

## Docs ownership

| File | Auto-update when |
|------|------------------|
| `docs/EPICS.md` | Epic starts / completes |
| `CHANGELOG.md` | Epic completes |
| `README.md` | Only if user-facing run/deploy steps change |

## Comments policy

- Comment **why**, not what the code already says.
- Public/service entry points may have a one-line purpose comment.
- Do not add changelog-style comments inside source files.
